{"parentUuid":"759fc881-ba2a-4e74-bf52-b46b64169022","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01AS674YjPkTszXrX129DTVA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GsnA5HgXHjdKuZ4hN9vDLg","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"MARCO MUS enumeration algorithm 2024 multiple minimal unsatisfiable subsets"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8082,"cache_read_input_tokens":13113,"output_tokens":417,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8082},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":417,"cache_read_input_tokens":13113,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8082,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaosyvSmC1q6o8aT1mLx1","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0897bc59-1195-4796-9aa6-d5478fc80d23","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:05.910Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0cc0dc3f-df22-4dfb-986d-1b7fa8234f11","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CieADpFFTBymZJ27zMTTqo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"QuickXplain QXP algorithm MUS minimal unsatisfiable subset Junker 2004 implementation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A formal proof and simple explanation of the QuickXplain algorithm - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9622537/\"},{\"title\":\"A formal proof and simple explanation of the QuickXplain algorithm | Artificial Intelligence Review | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10462-022-10149-w\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Understanding the QuickXPlain Algorithm: Simple Explanation and Formal Proof\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/338447151_Understanding_the_QuickXPlain_Algorithm_Simple_Explanation_and_Formal_Proof\"},{\"title\":\"A formal proof and simple explanation of the QuickXplain algorithm | Artificial Intelligence Review\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1007/s10462-022-10149-w\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A formal proof and simple explanation of the QuickXplain algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359807938_A_formal_proof_and_simple_explanation_of_the_QuickXplain_algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Understanding the QuickXPlain Algorithm: Simple Explanation and Formal Proof\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/66005629/Understanding_the_QuickXPlain_Algorithm_Simple_Explanation_and_Formal_Proof\"},{\"title\":\"Algorithms for Computing Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets of Constraints | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220532549_Algorithms_for_Computing_Minimal_Unsatisfiable_Subsets_of_Constraints\"},{\"title\":\"QUICKXPLAIN: Preferred explanations and relaxations for over-constrained problems | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/221607085_QUICKXPLAIN_Preferred_explanations_and_relaxations_for_over-constrained_problems\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) QuickXPlain: Conflict Detection for Arbitrary Constraint Propagation Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2395530_QuickXPlain_Conflict_Detection_for_Arbitrary_Constraint_Propagation_Algorithms\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) AMUSE: A minimally-unsatisfiable subformula extractor\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/4086492_AMUSE_A_minimally-unsatisfiable_subformula_extractor\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's comprehensive information about the QuickXplain algorithm and MUS:\n\n## Overview\n\nIn his seminal paper of 2004, Ulrich Junker proposed the QuickXplain algorithm, which provides a divide-and-conquer computation strategy to find within a given set an irreducible subset with a particular (monotone) property.\n\n## Applications\n\nThe problem of finding a MUS (Minimal Unsatisfiable Subset) is a manifestation of the MSMP (Minimal Set Subject to a Monotone Predicate) problem. Beside its original application in the domain of constraint satisfaction problems, the algorithm has since then found widespread adoption in areas as different as model-based diagnosis, recommender systems, verification, or the Semantic Web.\n\n## Algorithm Characteristics\n\nThis popularity is due to the frequent occurrence of the problem of finding irreducible subsets on the one hand, and to QuickXPlain's general applicability and favorable computational complexity on the other hand.\n\n## MUS Context\n\nOne task of interest expressible as an MSMP problem is to find a minimal unsatisfiable subset (MUS) of these clauses (which can help, e.g., to understand the cause of the clauses' inconsistency).\n\n## Implementation Details\n\nThe algorithm improves the efficiency of direct non-intrusive conflict detectors by recursively partitioning the problem into subproblems of half the size and by immediately skipping those subproblems that do not contain an element of the conflict.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4e267134-7b22-4831-bbea-bc2f0d61dfc9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:08.561Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0cc0dc3f-df22-4dfb-986d-1b7fa8234f11","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"af428c72-7a26-44c8-9ef1-6c1b6455a394","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HLsYht13JmtDxr3ukweEbB","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CPMpy mus tools.explain OR-Tools backend implementation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CPMpy ortools interface (cpmpy.solvers.ortools) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/minizinc_installed_subsolver/api/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy.solvers.ortools — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/_modules/cpmpy/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"Modeling and solving with CPMpy — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/modeling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Obtaining multiple solutions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/multiple_solutions.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpmpy/\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy/docs/multiple_solutions.md at master · CPMpy/cpmpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy/blob/master/docs/multiple_solutions.md\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - CPMpy/cpmpy: Constraint Programming and Modeling library in Python, based on numpy, with direct solver access. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy\"},{\"title\":\"CPMpy ortools interface (cpmpy.solvers.ortools) — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/min_max_bug/api/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · CPMpy/cpmpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy/releases\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy/cpmpy/solvers/ortools.py at master · CPMpy/cpmpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy/blob/master/cpmpy/solvers/ortools.py\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about CPMpy's OR-Tools backend implementation. Here's what I found:\n\n## CPMpy OR-Tools Backend Implementation\n\nCPMpy provides an interface to OR-Tools' CP-SAT Python API, with the 'ortools' python package bundled by default with CPMpy.\n\n### Overview\n\nGoogle OR-Tools is open source software for combinatorial optimization, and the OR-Tools CP-SAT solver is an award-winning constraint programming solver that uses SAT (satisfiability) methods and lazy-clause generation.\n\n### Key Features\n\n**Solver Class**: The main class is `cpmpy.solvers.ortools.CPM_ortools`\n\n**Variable Mapping**: CPMpy creates solver variables for CPMpy variables or returns from cache if previously created\n\n**Numeric Expression Support**: OR-Tools accepts decision variables, linear expressions with sum operations, and weighted sum operations\n\n**Assumptions and UNSAT Cores**: For repeated solving and use with get_core(): if the model is UNSAT, get_core() returns a small subset of assumption variables that are unsat together.\n\n**Stateless Interface**: The or-tools interface is stateless, so you can incrementally call solve() with assumptions, but or-tools will always start from scratch\n\n### Solver Parameters\n\nThe ortools solver parameters are defined in its 'sat_parameters.proto' description and can be used as keyword arguments to solve()\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e17024f5-860f-47de-afbd-e34ea4ccdb5a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:10.763Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"af428c72-7a26-44c8-9ef1-6c1b6455a394","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"51023423-a9b3-4490-aaf7-100c3eaef980","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U85MV8i9VJXHhCT2nNnV7i","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CP-SAT SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility minimal core extraction\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Solving with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/linearize-power/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization with Python: Infeasibility Explanation for Integer Programming with Google OR Tools | by Zia | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@ZiaH919/optimization-with-python-infeasibility-explanation-for-integer-programming-with-or-tools-7372b64322d\"},{\"title\":\"python cp-sat unsat assumption bug, is sat · Issue #2649 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2649\"},{\"title\":\"Assumptions\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/qlVv2uSq1uo\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumption variables — CPMpy 0.9.21 ...\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/unsat_core_extraction.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about OR-Tools CP-SAT and `SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility` for minimal core extraction:\n\n## Overview\n\nSince version 8.2, OR-Tools supports declaring assumptions, and extracting an unsat core. By enabling constraints conditionally using only_enforce_if(b) and adding b as an assumption, one can isolate sources of infeasibility. If the model proves infeasible, CP-SAT can return a minimal subset of the assumptions, and thus the corresponding constraints, that lead to the conflict.\n\n## The SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility Method\n\nThe `solver.SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility()` method displays infeasible literals, which represent the infeasible constraints.\n\n## Important Limitation\n\nHowever, there's a critical caveat: It is documented that it is reduced by heuristics, and not necessary minimal. If you want a minimal set of assumptions, do not use this API, just define the objective as the sum of literals. This means that `SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility()` does not guarantee finding the truly minimal core—it uses heuristics to find a reduced set of assumptions.\n\n## Alternative Approach\n\nThe workaround involves adding a boolean variable (called enforcement literal) corresponding to each constraint, which suggests if the constraint is enforced or not. The CP-SAT solver will heuristically find a (local) minimum assignment of false enforcement literals to get a feasible model, and you can then check which enforcement literal is False to find which constraint is infeasible/conflicting with others.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3905e170-93ea-4302-8506-f3ddc2858553","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:11.704Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"51023423-a9b3-4490-aaf7-100c3eaef980","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"759fc881-ba2a-4e74-bf52-b46b64169022","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013xcTLdQfGpJRZtPuToC7yZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PySAT musx MUSX API constraint MUS extraction example\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A deletion-based MUS extractor (pysat.examples.musx) — PySAT 1.8.dev26 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/examples/musx.html\"},{\"title\":\"pysat/examples/musx.py at master · pysathq/pysat\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat/blob/master/examples/musx.py\"},{\"title\":\"pysat/musx.py at b630cc7a9884a0ad1827e3add2935e309f862754 · pysathq/pysat\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat/blob/b630cc7a9884a0ad1827e3add2935e309f862754/examples/musx.py/\"},{\"title\":\"OptUx optimal MUS enumerator (pysat.examples.optux) — PySAT 1.9.dev1 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/examples/optux.html\"},{\"title\":\"Example Implementations | pysathq/pysathq.github.io | DeepWiki\",\"url\":\"https://deepwiki.com/pysathq/pysathq.github.io/4-example-implementations\"},{\"title\":\"MUS extraction unsupported for CNF with either OptUX and MUSx ? · Issue #162 · pysathq/pysat\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat/issues/162\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - pysathq/pysat: A toolkit for SAT-based prototyping in Python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat\"},{\"title\":\"PySAT: A Python Toolkit for Prototyping with SAT Oracles ⋆\",\"url\":\"https://alexeyignatiev.github.io/assets/pdf/imms-sat18-preprint.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PySAT: SAT technology in Python\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"pysat/docs/index.rst at master · pysathq/pysat\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat/blob/master/docs/index.rst\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found comprehensive information about PySAT's MUSX API for constraint-based MUS (Minimal Unsatisfiable Subset) extraction.\n\n## Overview\n\nMUSX is a MUS eXtractor using the deletion-based algorithm. The algorithm can be seen as an iterative process, which tries to remove one soft clause at a time and check whether the remaining set of soft clauses is still unsatisfiable together with the hard clauses.\n\n## API Usage Example\n\nThe algorithm can be accessed through the standard import interface of Python with:\n\n```python\nfrom pysat.examples.musx import MUSX\nfrom pysat.formula import WCNF\n\nwcnf = WCNF(from_file='formula.wcnf')\n\nmusx = MUSX(wcnf, verbosity=0)\nmusx.compute() # compute a minima\n```\n\n## Constructor Details\n\nThe constructor of MUSX objects receives a target CNF or WCNF formula, a SAT solver name, and a verbosity level. The default SAT solver is MiniSat22 (referred to as 'm22'). The default verbosity level is 1.\n\n## Algorithm Details\n\nThe following extraction procedure is implemented: oracle tests assumptions iteratively, removing one soft clause at a time and checking if the remaining clauses are still unsatisfiable, returning the minimal unsatisfiable set.\n\n## Command-line Usage\n\nThe implementation can be used as an executable with: `musx.py -s glucose3 -vv formula.wcnf`, which produces output showing the MUS approximation, tested clause IDs, and the final MUS result.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"96e815a1-b88c-48fb-ac05-44183c483904","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:13.538Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"759fc881-ba2a-4e74-bf52-b46b64169022","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0897bc59-1195-4796-9aa6-d5478fc80d23","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GsnA5HgXHjdKuZ4hN9vDLg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MARCO MUS enumeration algorithm 2024 multiple minimal unsatisfiable subsets\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Recursive Online Enumeration of All Minimal Unsatisﬁable Subsets\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1708.00400\"},{\"title\":\"MUST: Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets Enumeration Tool - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7439739/\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Pruning for Enumeration of Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v238/lymperopoulos24a/lymperopoulos24a.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Fast, flexible MUS enumeration | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/276905908_Fast_flexible_MUS_enumeration\"},{\"title\":\"Approximate Counting of Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets?\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~meel/Papers/cav20-bm.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Minimal Sets over a Monotone Predicate\",\"url\":\"https://jar-ben.github.io/papers/dissertationRevised.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"On the Superiority of Conﬂict-Driven Search in MUS Enumeration\",\"url\":\"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2289/paper14.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"arXiv:2507.03929v1 [cs.AI] 5 Jul 2025 TPLP: Page 1–8.\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.03929\"},{\"title\":\"Using Minimal Correction Sets to More Efficiently Compute ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.cs.toronto.edu/~fbacchus/csc2512/Readings/Bacchus-Katsirelos2015_Chapter_UsingMinimalCorrectionSetsToMo.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Exploiting Symmetries in MUS Computation\",\"url\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/33209/35364\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's information about MARCO and MUS enumeration algorithms:\n\n## MARCO Algorithm\n\nMARCO (short for Mapping Regions of Constraints) is an algorithm that identifies the frontier between satisfiable and unsatisfiable subsets of constraints. The symbolic representation used in MARCO was originally proposed by Liffiton et al. in the context of MUS enumeration and is used in their algorithm MARCO.\n\n## Multiple Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets (MUSes)\n\nA minimal unsatisfiable subset is a set of constraints that are not simultaneously satisfiable, yet the elimination of any of them makes the set satisfiable. Since there can be up to exponentially many MUSes, their complete enumeration might be intractable. Therefore, algorithms focus on enumerating MUSes online, one by one, and thus can find at least some MUSes even in the intractable cases.\n\n## Recent Developments (2024 and Beyond)\n\nThe search results show several recent approaches to MUS enumeration:\n\n1. **Graph Pruning for MUS Enumeration**: GRAPE-MUST enables the discovery of more MUSes. MARCO and REMUS maintain a small improvement across all formulas.\n\n2. **MUS Enumeration with Answer Set Programming**: A 2024 study evaluated MUS enumeration tools including MARCO, UNIMUS, and ReMUS.\n\n3. **Symmetry Exploitation**: Recent work in 2024 has studied symmetry-handling in constraint satisfaction problems, which has barely been studied in the context of MUS computation.\n\nThe search results indicate that MARCO remains a foundational algorithm for MUS enumeration, though newer approaches like ReMUS and specialized tools continue to improve upon its performance.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c33f1f90-852d-4181-ac36-3a377b77dd1c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:13.904Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0897bc59-1195-4796-9aa6-d5478fc80d23","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
