{"parentUuid":"3be7aae7-11a8-4d45-8d96-3fc9b0a91d66","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01STZGqfMUVYtCwkmEWHM3Hm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GhRLoHqRRreGf9YfWsYsez","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"combinatorial benders cuts minimal infeasible subset CP-SAT logic-based"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7357,"cache_read_input_tokens":21195,"output_tokens":611,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7357},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":611,"cache_read_input_tokens":21195,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7357,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7357,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caot1AkGcwey1kSwsyagG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f3a529b4-3af5-49b1-a646-da4a9d5ed93d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:22.930Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ab947cca-7cd2-474b-a8bc-f26ef954f5cb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UihXYW8zExm8p82bB8EHU7","type":"tool_result","content":"# CPMpy MUS Extraction with OR-Tools\n\n## Core API Functions\n\n**Primary functions for assumption-based solving:**\n\n1. **`s.solve(assumptions=[...])`** - Solves model with declared assumption literals\n2. **`s.get_core()`** - Extracts unsatisfiable core after unsatisfiable solve\n3. **`mus(m.constraints)`** - Computes Minimal Unsatisfiable Subset using deletion algorithm\n\n## Code Example\n\nThe documentation provides this representative example:\n\n```python\nfrom cpmpy import *\nfrom cpmpy.solvers import CPM_ortools\n\nbv = boolvar(shape=3)\niv = intvar(0,9, shape=3)\n\nm = Model(\n    bv[0].implies(iv[0] > iv[1]),\n    bv[1].implies(iv[1] > iv[2]),\n    bv[2].implies(iv[2] > iv[0])\n)\n\ns = CPM_ortools(m)\nprint(s.solve(assumptions=bv))\nprint(s.status())\nprint(\"core:\", s.get_core())\n```\n\n## Performance Characteristics\n\nThe documentation notes a significant limitation: *\"the default solver's Python interface is by design stateless\"*—meaning repeated calls with different assumptions don't preserve learnt clauses, warm-starting, or incrementality, causing \"pre-processing overhead.\"\n\n**Workaround:** `s.solution_hint()` enables manual warm-starting with previously found solutions.\n\n## Additional Tools\n\nThe `cpmpy.tools` module includes `mus` for deletion-based MUS extraction, plus implementations of MARCO algorithm and stepwise explanation algorithms for SAT problems."}]},"uuid":"90786114-4f82-42d1-9901-dd587dfcf076","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:23.188Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ab947cca-7cd2-474b-a8bc-f26ef954f5cb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"32ce2bbf-d587-43bf-bda1-76c30e7be19d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015kvCpMdohmiGCzNPTx3xfu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"QuickXplain divide conquer algorithm pseudocode constraint Junker complexity log\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A formal proof and simple explanation of the QuickXplain algorithm | Artificial Intelligence Review | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10462-022-10149-w\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 2 Divide-and-conquer algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~vazirani/algorithms/chap2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Divide-and-conquer algorithm - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divide-and-conquer_algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"A formal proof and simple explanation of the QuickXplain algorithm | Artificial Intelligence Review\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1007/s10462-022-10149-w\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Divide and Conquer Algorithm - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/introduction-to-divide-and-conquer-algorithm/\"},{\"title\":\"Divide and Conquer Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://www.enjoyalgorithms.com/blog/divide-and-conquer/\"},{\"title\":\"Divide and Conquer Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://www.programiz.com/dsa/divide-and-conquer\"},{\"title\":\"Divide and conquer algorithms (article)\",\"url\":\"https://www.khanacademy.org/computing/computer-science/algorithms/merge-sort/a/divide-and-conquer-algorithms\"},{\"title\":\"Divide and Conquer Algorithm Meaning: Explained with Examples\",\"url\":\"https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/divide-and-conquer-algorithms/\"},{\"title\":\"DAA 2019 2. Divide and Conquer Algorithms – 1 / 52\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/files/12487/divide-and-conquer.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about QuickXplain:\n\nQuickXplain is an algorithm proposed by Ulrich Junker in 2004 that provides a divide-and-conquer computation strategy to find within a given set an irreducible subset with a particular (monotone) property.\n\n## Algorithm Characteristics\n\nQuickXplain uses a divide-and-conquer strategy that splits sets into equally-sized subsets. The algorithm has found widespread adoption in areas such as model-based diagnosis, recommender systems, verification, and the Semantic Web.\n\n## Complexity\n\nQuickXplain is popular partly due to its mild theoretical complexity in terms of the number of (usually expensive) predicate evaluations required, as well as its favorable practical performance for important problems such as conflict or diagnosis computation for model-based diagnosis.\n\n## Original Application\n\nThe algorithm's original application was in the domain of constraint satisfaction problems.\n\nUnfortunately, the search results do not contain specific pseudocode or detailed logarithmic complexity bounds for the QuickXplain algorithm. For more detailed technical information about the algorithm's pseudocode and complexity analysis, you may want to refer to Junker's original 2004 paper or the formal proof paper from Artificial Intelligence Review (2022) that was mentioned in the search results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f69bf226-5b13-41b4-9666-df8d06606159","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:28.189Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"32ce2bbf-d587-43bf-bda1-76c30e7be19d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"4c996a9b-0d1a-4df6-a5c1-324ee1c55303","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012kve4G7P7g5mPdvLR9Vho3","type":"tool_result","content":"# PySAT MUSX Deletion-Based MUS Algorithm\n\n## API Signature\n```python\nMUSX(formula, solver='m22', verbosity=1)\nmusx.compute()  # returns list of clause IDs\n```\n\n## Core Algorithm\nThe implementation uses an iterative deletion approach: \"tries to remove one soft clause at a time and check whether the remaining set of soft clauses is still unsatisfiable together with the hard clauses.\"\n\nThe pseudocode loop processes assumptions sequentially, testing whether removing each clause maintains unsatisfiability until a minimal set remains.\n\n## Incremental SAT/Assumptions\nThe algorithm leverages the MiniSat-like assumptions interface. Soft clauses are augmented with selector literals—each clause `c` becomes `(c ∨ ¬s)`—allowing activation via literal assumption. The unsatisfiable core is provided as a list of selector literals.\n\n## Default SAT Solver\nMiniSat22 (`'m22'`) is the default solver, though alternatives are available via the `solver` parameter.\n\n## Time Complexity & Performance\nNo explicit time complexity analysis is provided. The documentation notes this is a \"simplistic implementation\" and mentions \"oracle time\" reporting but omits specific performance characteristics.\n\n## Alternative Algorithms\n**No mention of QuickXplain or alternative MUS extraction algorithms** appears in this documentation excerpt. The module focuses exclusively on the deletion-based approach.\n\n## Limitations\nThe implementation handles only plain and partial CNF formulas (DIMACS, WCNF formats) and \"can compute only one MUS (MUS enumeration is not supported).\""}]},"uuid":"14da1cb8-6238-4d31-ae29-9e2d56509675","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:28.218Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4c996a9b-0d1a-4df6-a5c1-324ee1c55303","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3be7aae7-11a8-4d45-8d96-3fc9b0a91d66","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WW4zxRwQyzmwkghjoj6Fjz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"deletion based MUS incremental SAT assumptions speedup vs reset\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT and incremental usage under assumptions: possible?\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/n8u8yoF4UOI\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving with Assumptions: Application to MUS Extraction | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-39071-5_23\"},{\"title\":\"use minisat for incremental solving\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/minisat/c/90IQ-x9ovIk\"},{\"title\":\"Factoring Out Assumptions to Speed Up MUS Extraction | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-39071-5_21\"},{\"title\":\"Factoring Out Assumptions to Speed Up MUS Extraction⋆\",\"url\":\"https://cca.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/LagniezBiere-SAT13.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A deletion-based MUS extractor (pysat.examples.musx) — PySAT 1.8.dev26 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/examples/musx.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction MUS Compilation Incremental SAT and applications Gilles Audemard\",\"url\":\"http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/~audemard/slidesMontpellier2014.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving with ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/articles/xmain.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving with Assumptions: Application to MUS Extraction | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/262286933_Improving_Glucose_for_Incremental_SAT_Solving_with_Assumptions_Application_to_MUS_Extraction\"},{\"title\":\"Refactoring Varisat: 5. Incremental Solving and Proofs - jix.one\",\"url\":\"https://jix.one/refactoring-varisat-5-incremental-solving-and-proofs/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about deletion-based MUS extraction with incremental SAT solving and the speedup versus reset question:\n\n## Key Findings\n\nDeletion-based MUS extraction works by removing clauses from an over-approximation of a minimal unsatisfiable set and checking whether the remaining clauses are still unsatisfiable together with hard clauses.\n\n### Incremental SAT with Assumptions and Speedup\n\nThe point of incremental SAT solving is re-using learnt clauses. When working with assumptions for deletion-based extraction, there's a trade-off between maintaining incremental state and resetting:\n\nWith enough assumptions, at some point it becomes faster to switch to non-incremental solving instead, though there have been attempts to overcome this problem.\n\n### Managing Learned Clauses\n\nResearch has focused on optimizing which learned clauses to keep after each incremental SAT call. After an incremental SAT call returns \"unsatisfiable\", researchers propose keeping only learned clauses that were used to show inconsistency of assumptions and discarding others, as exactly those learned clauses related to the last set of assumptions are likely useful in the next SAT call.\n\nBy playing on clause database cleaning, assumptions management, and other classical parameters, incremental SAT solvers can significantly improve deletion-based MUS extraction.\n\nThe search results show that this is an active area of research in SAT solver optimization, with work on the Glucose and MiniSat solvers specifically addressing these performance issues in MUS extraction.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d2c48058-2016-4ad8-8700-a850c958fb9b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:29.706Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3be7aae7-11a8-4d45-8d96-3fc9b0a91d66","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f3a529b4-3af5-49b1-a646-da4a9d5ed93d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GhRLoHqRRreGf9YfWsYsez","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"combinatorial benders cuts minimal infeasible subset CP-SAT logic-based\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Combinatorial Benders Cuts\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/combinatorial-benders-cuts\"},{\"title\":\"Combinatorial Benders’ Cuts | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-25960-2_14\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/logic-based-benders-decomposition-lbbd-4e47b3ae-690a-44a4-9d80-a191b385b4e6\"},{\"title\":\"Solving a Selective Dial-a-Ride Problem with Logic-based ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ac.tuwien.ac.at/files/tr/ac-tr-16-007.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Combinatorial Benders' Cuts for Mixed-Integer Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://ideas.repec.org/a/inm/oropre/v54y2006i4p756-766.html\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Combinatorial Benders' Cuts for Mixed-Integer Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220244181_Combinatorial_Benders'_Cuts_for_Mixed-Integer_Linear_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-030-54621-2_903-1\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Combinatorial Benders’ Cuts\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/221316776_Combinatorial_Benders'_Cuts\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition - John Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/encyLBBD.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Combinatorial Benders' Cuts for Mixed-Integer Linear Programming | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Combinatorial-Benders'-Cuts-for-Mixed-Integer-Codato-Fischetti/7ba61b0c68ee3b4557d888146f0f109593d96470\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about combinatorial Benders cuts with minimal infeasible subsets and logic-based approaches:\n\n## Combinatorial Benders Cuts Overview\n\nCombinatorial Benders cuts are polyhedral constraints derived from explicit combinatorial analysis of discrete problem structure within Benders decomposition frameworks. In contrast to classical (duality-based) Benders cuts, which leverage the dual information of continuous or LP subproblems, combinatorial Benders cuts exploit primal infeasibility, logic, or minimal combinatorial conflict to produce strong, problem-specific valid inequalities.\n\n## Minimal Infeasible Subsets in Cut Generation\n\nOne core class of combinatorial Benders cuts targets infeasibility by explicitly identifying (and cutting off) minimal infeasible sets (MIS). The inequalities are associated to minimal (or irreducible) infeasible subsystems of a certain linear system, and can be separated efficiently in case the master solution is integer.\n\n## Logic-Based Approaches\n\nWhile the classical Benders cut is obtained via LP dual multipliers, LBBD relies on combinatorial or logical inference extracted from subproblem solutions. Two essential categories of cuts in LBBD are: Nogood (no-good) or Exclusion Cuts that encode when the current master assignment leads to infeasibility in the subproblem, where a minimal infeasible subsystem (IIS) or concrete \"nogood\" pattern is extracted and used to cut off that assignment.\n\n## Connection to CP-SAT\n\nThese cuts have demonstrated significant computational impact across integer and mixed-integer programs, logic-based Benders paradigms, stochastic and robust optimization, and high-dimensional combinatorial applications. In LBBD, the master problem and subproblems can be solved using heterogeneous optimization technologies (e.g., MIP, CP, MILP, specialized algorithmics), with logic-based inference used for generating valid cuts.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"b1b138e4-fb2a-4eb6-99b1-b90e6733faad","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:31.998Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f3a529b4-3af5-49b1-a646-da4a9d5ed93d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
