{"parentUuid":"ef0be187-ac4a-4d98-b4d8-49a40e4dd655","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019AecMNm1svkdv6gZifJYaN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016wvvy9u9tDp5XSZNHNdxk9","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://cca.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/LagniezBiere-SAT13.pdf","prompt":"Extract specific speedup numbers for deletion-based MUS with incremental SAT versus reset approach. Also mention the assumption factoring technique and its performance gains."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5885,"cache_read_input_tokens":28552,"output_tokens":524,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5885},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":524,"cache_read_input_tokens":28552,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5885,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5885,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caot2XVU8pnoeLLRaBhct","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e46e6780-530c-4b34-9319-48c0200f8469","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:40.351Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7c39cbd0-b40e-4620-b74e-11711c2b7753","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018MaTJ8SHd9JSi1pK4fVDVN","type":"tool_result","content":"# MUS Algorithms in CPMpy\n\n## Main Algorithms Implemented\n\n### 1. **mus()** - Assumption-Based Deletion MUS\n**Function Signature:**\n```python\nmus(soft, hard=[], solver=\"ortools\")\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `soft`: soft constraints (list of expressions)\n- `hard`: hard constraints (optional)\n- `solver`: must support assumptions (ortools, exact, z3, pysat)\n\n**Approach:** Uses \"assumption variables and unsat core extraction.\" Extracts an unsatisfiable core, then shrinks it by iteratively omitting one assumption variable. Constraints with many variables are prioritized for removal.\n\n### 2. **quickxplain()** - Preference-Based MUS\n**Function Signature:**\n```python\nquickxplain(soft, hard=[], solver=\"ortools\")\n```\n\n**Approach:** Finds \"a preferred minimal unsatisfiable subset based on constraint ordering.\" Lower-indexed constraints are preferred. Based on Junker's 2004 algorithm using recursive splitting.\n\n### 3. **ocus()** / **optimal_mus()** - Weighted Optimal MUS\n**Function Signature:**\n```python\noptimal_mus(soft, hard=[], weights=None, solver=\"ortools\", hs_solver=\"ortools\")\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `weights`: linear objective weights (None defaults to smallest MUS)\n- `meta_constraint`: Boolean CPMpy expression with soft constraints\n\n**Approach:** Iteratively generates correction subsets and computes optimal hitting sets.\n\n### 4. **mus_native()** - Solver-Specific Implementation\n**Function Signature:**\n```python\nmus_native(soft, hard=[], solver=\"exact\")\n```\n\nUses native solver extractors (exact or Gurobi IIS-based).\n\n## Naive Variants\n\nNon-assumption-based versions for solvers lacking assumption support:\n- `mus_naive()` - deletion-based, \"re-solves from scratch\"\n- `quickxplain_naive()`\n- `ocus_naive()` / `optimal_mus_naive()`\n\n## Complexity Notes\n\nThe code indicates naive variants are \"terribly slow\" for non-trivial constraint sets due to repeated full model resolution."}]},"uuid":"fe61c72b-3110-4577-94bc-21a80343a825","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:44.307Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7c39cbd0-b40e-4620-b74e-11711c2b7753","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"324344ac-6e94-4d2d-98d2-70c3ef79e820","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019mB2RKEnotgX9ZfHfXf1GX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cpmpy.tools.explain mus quickxplain implementation github\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Explanation tools (cpmpy.tools.explain) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/simpl_bool_fix/api/tools/explain.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - CPMpy/XCP-explain: Explainable Constraint Solving - A Hands-On Tutorial · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/XCP-explain\"},{\"title\":\"Solving with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"CPMpy · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cpmpy\"},{\"title\":\"CPMpy Release 0.9.24 Tias Guns Oct 06, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/_/downloads/en/latest/pdf/\"},{\"title\":\"Explainable Constraint Solving - A Hands-On Tutorial | XCP-explain\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.github.io/XCP-explain/\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumption variables — CPMpy 0.9.21 ...\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/z3-auto-subsolver/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy/examples/advanced/musx.py at master · CPMpy/cpmpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CPMpy/cpmpy/blob/master/examples/advanced/musx.py\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/complex_sudoku_examples/unsat_core_extraction.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about `cpmpy.tools.explain` MUS and QuickXplain implementation:\n\n## CPMpy's Explanation Tools\n\nCPMpy includes a re-implementation of MUS-computation techniques, which are available in the `cpmpy.tools.explain` module.\n\n## QuickXplain Implementation\n\nThe `quickxplain` function finds a preferred minimal unsatisfiable subset of constraints based on the ordering of constraints, with a total order imposed using the ordering of soft constraints. It uses an assumption-based implementation for solvers that support `s.solve(assumptions=…)` and `s.get_core()`.\n\nThe parameters include soft constraints (to find a preferred minimal unsatisfiable subset of), hard constraints (added to the model before solving), and a solver name that must support assumptions (e.g., \"ortools\", \"exact\", \"z3\" or \"pysat\").\n\nCPMpy's implementation is based on the QuickXplain algorithm by Junker from \"Preferred explanations and relaxations for over-constrained problems\" (AAAI-2004).\n\n## Additional MUS Methods\n\nCPMpy also includes a deletion-based MUS algorithm using assumption variables and unsat core extraction, which extracts an unsat core and then shrinks the core further by repeatedly omitting one assumption variable.\n\nFor more information and hands-on examples, you can visit the XCP-explain repository at https://github.com/CPMpy/XCP-explain.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"030d15ba-6243-4fde-9073-c6fa9790929e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:44.824Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"324344ac-6e94-4d2d-98d2-70c3ef79e820","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ef0be187-ac4a-4d98-b4d8-49a40e4dd655","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a3bef849bbe8777ab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VKzHujQrrHtMjrqWwyXsyh","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility\" or-tools example python AddAssumption\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Assumptions\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/qlVv2uSq1uo\"},{\"title\":\"python cp-sat unsat assumption bug, is sat · Issue #2649 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2649\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization with Python: Infeasibility Explanation for Integer Programming with Google OR Tools | by Zia | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@ZiaH919/optimization-with-python-infeasibility-explanation-for-integer-programming-with-or-tools-7372b64322d\"},{\"title\":\"SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility in C#/.Net returns the original set. · Issue #3983 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3983\"},{\"title\":\"Solution hint and Assumptions reports wrong status when solved after presolve · Issue #4324 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4324\"},{\"title\":\"Strange interaction between assumptions interface and using more than one search worker · Issue #2563 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2563\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools: ortools/sat/docs/troubleshooting.md | Fossies\",\"url\":\"https://fossies.org/linux/or-tools/ortools/sat/docs/troubleshooting.md\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT get's stuck when trying to maximize sum of boolean variables that are all added as assumptions · google/or-tools · Discussion #3630\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/discussions/3630\"},{\"title\":\"Infeasable assumptions are not reported · Issue #4109 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4109\"},{\"title\":\"Get Started with OR-Tools for Python | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/introduction/python\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about using `SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility` and `AddAssumption` in OR-Tools with Python:\n\n## Usage Examples\n\nA basic example from OR-Tools shows creating a model with variables x, y, z and boolean variables a, b, c, adding constraints with `only_enforce_if()` to make them conditional, then using `add_assumptions([a, b, c])` before solving with a CpSolver.\n\n## How SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility Works\n\nAfter solving an infeasible model with assumptions, you can call `solver.SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility()` to get infeasible variables, then retrieve the actual variable using `model.GetBoolVarFromProtoIndex(i)`.\n\n## Important Limitations\n\nThe `SufficientAssumptionsForInfeasibility` method will eventually give a \"small\" set of assumptions in case of infeasibility, but it is not fully implemented yet. Currently, it will minimize the set only in a single thread and without an objective, otherwise you basically get all of the assumptions.\n\nThe result is reduced by heuristics and not necessarily minimal. If you want a minimal set of assumptions, you should not use this API, but instead define the objective as the sum of literals.\n\n## Incompatibility with Objectives\n\nHaving an objective in a model is incompatible with assumptions, which is an important constraint to remember when designing your model.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"37118416-147f-4e3e-a840-bc977ae82b1b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:59:45.698Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ef0be187-ac4a-4d98-b4d8-49a40e4dd655","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
